# Metaphysics: The Nature of Reality

> When a physicist says only particles and fields exist, a theist
> says souls do, a mathematician insists numbers do — metaphysics is
> the discipline that referees. It asks what exists, what existence
> even means, and what holds the world together: substance, cause,
> time, person, free will. The other sciences presuppose its answers;
> metaphysics makes them explicit.

## 1. What Metaphysics Asks

[definition]
**Metaphysics** is the philosophical study of the fundamental nature
of reality — what exists (ontology), what things are (essence,
identity), and how they relate (space, time, causation, modality).
Its name (literally "after the Physics") comes from the ordering of
Aristotle's books, not from any supernaturalism.
[/definition]

Core questions: What is there? What is a *thing*? Do properties
exist or only particulars? Is change real or illusory? Are time and
space containers or relations? Am I my body, my mind, or a pattern?
Could the world have been otherwise — and in what sense?

## 2. Ontology: The Inventory of Being

- **Materialism/physicalism** — only physical stuff exists; minds
  are what brains do. The default of contemporary science.
- **Idealism** — reality is fundamentally mental (Berkeley: *esse est
  percipi*, to be is to be perceived; the universe is ideas in
  minds, ultimately God's).
- **Dualism** — both matter and mind exist as distinct substances
  (Descartes' *res cogitans* and *res extensa*); beset by the
  interaction problem: how do ghost and machine touch?
- **Neutral monism** — one neutral stuff underlies both matter-talk
  and mind-talk (Russell, Spinoza's one substance with two
  attributes).
- **Nihilism/eliminativism** — some categories are empty: perhaps
  tables, or even beliefs, do not exist in the strict sense.

**Universals and particulars.** Is *redness* one thing present in
every red object (Platonic realism), a resemblance among particulars
(nominalism), or a mind-made bundle (conceptualism)? The medieval
problem of universals is still the cleanest introduction to
ontology's method.

## 3. Substance, Essence and Identity

Aristotle analysed things as **substance + properties** (this horse
and its whiteness), with a fourfold theory of **causes**: material,
formal, efficient and final. Essential properties make a thing what
it is; accidental ones it could lose.

**Identity over time** — the Ship of Theseus: if every plank is
replaced, is it the same ship? Personal identity sharpens the
puzzle: are you the same person as the seven-year-old because of
bodily continuity, psychological continuity (Locke's memory
chains), or neither (Buddhist anatta / Hume's bundle theory — the
self is just a bundle of perceptions with no owner)?

## 4. Space, Time and Change

| Position | Claim | Champion |
| --- | --- | --- |
| Absolutism | space/time are containers existing independently | Newton, Clarke |
| Relationism | space/time are systems of relations among things | Leibniz, Mach |
| Eternalism | past, present, future all equally real ("block universe") | Putnam, Minkowski |
| Presentism | only the present exists | Aristotle (arguably), many common-sensists |

Zeno's paradoxes (Achilles and the tortoise; the arrow frozen at
each instant) still structure debates about continuity, infinity and
whether motion is an appearance. McTaggart's A-series/B-series
distinction (time as flowing vs time as an ordering) powers the
modern philosophy of time; entropy gives physics its arrow.

## 5. Causation and Modality

Hume saw causation as nothing over and above constant conjunction —
we observe *A-after-B*, never a necessary link; Kant answered that
causality is a category the mind imposes. Regularity, counterfactual
(if the match hadn't been struck, no flame), and interventionist
(doing A yields B) theories compete today — the stakes are both
metaphysical and methodological.

**Modality** — necessity and possibility. Is a necessary truth true
in all possible worlds (Lewis's modal realism takes worlds as
concretely real; actualism demotes them to useful fictions)? Could
water have failed to be H₂O? Kripke's necessary *a posteriori*
truths (water is H₂O — discovered empirically, necessarily true)
reshaped the whole field.

## 6. Minds, Free Will and Persons

**Free will versus determinism** — the hard problem's older
sibling:

- **Hard determinism** — every event, including choices, is fixed by
  prior causes; free will is illusion.
- **Libertarianism** — choices are genuinely undetermined; agents
  originate action.
- **Compatibilism** — freedom means acting per your own reasons
  without constraint, which is compatible with determinism (Hume,
  Frankfurt: what you'd want to want — second-order desires).
- **Hard incompatibilism** (Strawson, Pereboom) — either way, the
  strong desert-entailing freedom needed for ultimate blame does not
  exist.

Physics joins in: quantum indeterminacy helps libertarians not at
all (randomness is not agency), while Libet-style experiments about
readiness potentials remain contested.

**The hard problem of consciousness** (Chalmers) — why is there
something it is like to see red? Qualia, philosophical zombies and
Mary the colour scientist define the puzzle; physicalist replies
(functionalism, illusionism, representationalism) and panpsychist
escape routes compete.

## 7. Metaphysics Under Fire — and Rebuilt

Hume's fork and the logical positivists (Carnap, Ayer) dismissed
metaphysics as poetry in lab coats: unverifiable, meaningless.
Quine's naturalised philosophy and Kripke's *Naming and Necessity*
(1970s) revived it — modal logic, possible-worlds semantics and
analytic metaphysics (identity, persistence, events, grounding) made
the discipline rigorous again. Today it intersects physics (what is
a quantum field? does time flow?), biology (what is a species?) and
computer science (is a uploaded pattern *you*?).

## 8. Summary

Metaphysics is the audit of reality's inventory: what exists (mind,
matter, numbers, worlds), what persists (persons, ships), what
connects (cause, space, time) and what could be otherwise. Its great
battles — realism versus nominalism, absolutism versus relationism,
dualism versus physicalism, freedom versus determinism — persist
because each side captures real intuitions. To study them is to
learn what your own world-view quietly assumes; that is the
discipline's oldest service.
